    "freeTraits": 0,
    "biography": "<h2>Background</h2><p>Embraced in 1799 after French soldiers occupying Cairo used the mentally disturbed teenage Aziz for target practice, Aziz was saved at the brink of death by his sire, whose vitae carried into him a fragment of the essence of an ancient djinn. That fragment focused Aziz's shattered mind and set him upon a singular, lifelong contemplation of duality and the Eternal Riddle. In the two centuries since, he has become a beloved fixture of downtown Cairo, spinning riddles and rhyming verse (called <em>radha</em>, a beggars' cant) for coin and company on street corners and in coffeehouses, known to Kindred and kine alike.</p><p>Unbeknownst to nearly everyone in the city, Aziz has achieved Golconda. Every one of his seemingly nonsensical riddles contains genuine, often vital truth about the state of the City Triumphant, discernible to anyone who listens closely enough &mdash; a fact that makes him one of the best-informed and most quietly powerful Kindred in Cairo, hiding in plain sight as a harmless eccentric.</p><h2>Roleplaying Hints</h2><p>Speak only in playful, rhyming riddles, no matter the topic or stakes. Watch everyone constantly from behind a disarming smile; you already know more about the person in front of you than they realize they've given away.</p>",
    "notes": "Sire: Al-Basnyn Abyad. Has achieved Golconda; Storytellers who prefer not to use Golconda in their chronicle should reduce his Auspex rating and simply portray him as an unusually wise, sane @UUID[Compendium.vtmlarp.clans.JournalEntry.ZJx9H3XnU8yQGy9s]{Malkavian}. Longtime friend and theological conversation partner of Shahid (Ibn Ja'far's childe); intends to help Shahid's sire, or Shahid himself, toward Golconda as thanks for what he learned watching Shahid's example.",
    "npcType": "vampire"
  },
